WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:10.210
So now let's dive into the
best practices for developing

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:15.410
Shiny apps.

00:00:15.410 --> 00:00:17.830
And in my talk
I'm going to go over

00:00:17.830 --> 00:00:24.370
three areas, which is organizing
your Shiny app code, organizing

00:00:24.370 --> 00:00:29.110
your development environment,
and organizing your testing.

00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:32.350
Since we have a limited
amount of time,

00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:34.600
this will be a high
level overview talk.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:37.340
And I'm going to introduce
concepts and tools

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:40.000
you should be using
and tell you why.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:41.740
So if you are not
familiar with them,

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:45.740
you can explore them in
more details after the talk.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:54.890
So how should you organize
the code inside your Shiny app?

00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:58.940
Well, first answer is
use Shiny modules.

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:02.100
Shiny modules let you
decompose your application

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:07.940
so you can follow the Don't Repeat
Yourself coding principle.

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:11.930
They offer encapsulation,
modularity, reusability,

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:18.140
and allow you to test
the whole components.

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:22.400
They allow you to organize your
app in the server and the UI

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:27.440
part, which in
essence means that you

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:32.600
can define your own pair
of server and UI logic

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:38.920
and use it inside your app or
embedded in another module.

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:46.260
And so secondly, you can
also organize your code

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:49.610
using R6 classes.
and an R6 class

00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:53.960
is a modern, fast, and simple
implementation of

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:57.680
object oriented
programming in R.

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:02.750
You can look at it as a more
organized Shiny module,

00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:06.020
and R6 classes
introduces a clear system

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:10.669
of getting the current state of
the piece of the functionality,

00:02:10.669 --> 00:02:12.680
the operations that
can be performed

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:17.150
with it, the auxiliary functions, and
the initial state of the object

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:20.570
that can depend on the
particular user rights, which

00:02:20.570 --> 00:02:23.570
might be actually super useful.

00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:28.110
So how do you organize
your code using R6 classes?

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:31.370
So you can keep each
class in a separate script

00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:34.190
with a name similar
to the class name,

00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:38.660
you can store the scripts
in the folder separate

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:43.880
from the other code, and
you can input the code

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:47.690
using a package or as in
the example,

00:02:47.690 --> 00:02:50.340
using a use function
from the module.

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:55.550
Second, well, and then you
can initialize the class

00:02:55.550 --> 00:02:58.970
with a new method,
which is ultimately

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:04.550
available for all the classes,
and that's just an object.

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:14.600
And the first option is to
use modules from wahani.

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:19.670
And I would like to avoid some
confusion with Shiny modules.

00:03:19.670 --> 00:03:21.120
So the modules

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:24.830
I'm going to talk about right now is
something completely different

00:03:24.830 --> 00:03:27.140
from Shiny modules.

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:31.340
And so the modules are
the organizational unit

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:37.610
for the source code and they can
contain Shiny modules or R6

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:41.360
classes or just functions
you define.

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:44.690
And the cool thing about them
is that they enforce rigor

00:03:44.690 --> 00:03:47.750
when defining the dependencies.

00:03:47.750 --> 00:03:51.470
It means that you have
to explicitly declare

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:56.360
which functions or
packages your code is using

00:03:56.360 --> 00:03:58.010
and you have to import them.

00:03:58.010 --> 00:04:01.760
And you also have a full
control of the things that

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:05.540
are available outside
the module because you

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:08.930
have to export them
using the export function

00:04:08.930 --> 00:04:11.780
as shown here in the example.

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:14.900
And additionally, they
have a local search

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:17.779
and they can be used as
subunit within the package

00:04:17.779 --> 00:04:19.820
or in scripts.

00:04:19.820 --> 00:04:22.730
So to put it all
together, Shiny modules

00:04:22.730 --> 00:04:24.890
R6 classes, and modules.

00:04:24.890 --> 00:04:26.900
So Shiny modules,
they let you

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:31.390
reuse UI and the server parts of
the components. R6 classes,

00:04:31.390 --> 00:04:34.850
they let you build objects that
have a single responsibility

00:04:34.850 --> 00:04:37.970
and implement given
logic, and the webhani modules,

00:04:37.970 --> 00:04:41.210
they have selected
dependencies and let

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:45.260
you organize files that could
be Shiny modules and R6

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:48.380
classes as a separate unit.

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:52.230
So OK, we covered
organizing the code,

00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:53.310
so now

00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:57.200
let's talk about the
development environment

00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:03.180
and how you can organize it.

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:10.540
So continuous integration.
To ensure quality, automate

00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:15.910
checks. You can run Lint and test
automatically for every pushed change.

00:05:15.910 --> 00:05:18.210
And especially for
tests, you probably

00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:20.340
will forget them every time.

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:22.090
So just automate it.

00:05:22.090 --> 00:05:24.600
So you don't
have to think about it.

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:27.570
And additionally,
you can automatically

00:05:27.570 --> 00:05:30.930
add a pull request
template that can contain

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:35.070
a checklist for a pull request
creator and for the reviewer

00:05:35.070 --> 00:05:40.530
to every pull request, you can push,
you can block pushing directly

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:44.460
to master and block
pull requests,

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:46.310
merging pull requests if the test fails.

00:05:49.180 --> 00:05:53.380
So here are some popular
continuous integration options,

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:57.700
Bitbucket CircleCI, Travis,
Jenkins and GitHub actions,

00:05:57.700 --> 00:06:00.260
so I'm sure you will
find something that

00:06:00.260 --> 00:06:04.650
will fit your technology stack.

00:06:04.650 --> 00:06:09.520
And remember, you don't have to
start from scratch every time.

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:11.140
And you

00:06:11.140 --> 00:06:13.770
can use a product
template for it.

00:06:13.770 --> 00:06:17.520
So at Appsilon, we have
an internal product

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:22.210
out there with a Shiny
boiler plate template.

00:06:22.210 --> 00:06:25.930
And we use it to initialize
the repository structure

00:06:25.930 --> 00:06:30.360
if we start a new project. And
it contains a simple Shiny app

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:33.880
with modules and
Shiny modules set up.

00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:38.190
It's also has a simple, unique test.

00:06:38.190 --> 00:06:43.170
And the CI with Lint and
automatic unit tests.

00:06:46.790 --> 00:06:51.860
OK, so how actually your
development environment

00:06:51.860 --> 00:06:54.630
should look like.

00:06:54.630 --> 00:07:00.090
So let's start
from the other way.

00:07:00.090 --> 00:07:03.450
So what happens if you
don't take care of it?

00:07:03.450 --> 00:07:08.580
So your development environment is
a crucial part of working in R.

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:11.580
And it's not only
Shiny-specific,

00:07:11.580 --> 00:07:16.530
so otherwise, if you
don't take care of it,

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:19.020
you can end up
with the same code,

00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:21.640
giving different results
on different machines.

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:25.410
Other projects can be affected
as in the global environment

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:28.800
that can change and
crush unrelated projects.

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:32.040
You can imagine the deployment
is long and difficult.

00:07:32.040 --> 00:07:34.920
Establishing infrastructure
is challenging

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:36.510
if it's not being tracked.

00:07:36.510 --> 00:07:39.450
And last but not least,
the team will waste time

00:07:39.450 --> 00:07:43.200
setting up a new environment
rather than jumping straight

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:43.950
to work.

00:07:43.950 --> 00:07:46.660
So this might not be a problem
when you're starting a project,

00:07:46.660 --> 00:07:49.890
but imagine that you have
to urgently add another team

00:07:49.890 --> 00:07:52.980
member in the middle of one.

00:07:52.980 --> 00:08:00.010
So to solve all those problems,
we recommend following a setup.

00:08:00.010 --> 00:08:05.250
So you can do a development
in RStudio running inside

00:08:05.250 --> 00:08:08.970
a Docker container, which is fixed
and dedicated to your project.

00:08:08.970 --> 00:08:11.530
And you can leverage
Docker and the renv package

00:08:11.530 --> 00:08:15.210
to control the underlying
system dependencies and

00:08:15.210 --> 00:08:18.540
R packages.
Docker and renv together

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:20.940
make team collaboration easy.

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:25.480
So all the changes to the Docker
file and renv reports

00:08:25.480 --> 00:08:27.990
of the packages
used in the project

00:08:27.990 --> 00:08:34.860
are committed to the DockerHub repository
and to the code repository.

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:39.850
For example, GitHub is what our
team is using.

00:08:39.850 --> 00:08:42.789
On the other hand, the
previous solution still

00:08:42.789 --> 00:08:47.140
requires some level
of devops skills and what

00:08:47.140 --> 00:08:52.450
we observe with our clients,
especially the enterprise ones,

00:08:52.450 --> 00:08:56.350
they choose to work with
RStudio Server Pro, that

00:08:56.350 --> 00:08:59.530
allows their data science
team to purely focus

00:08:59.530 --> 00:09:01.750
on delivering value,
using their core

00:09:01.750 --> 00:09:06.140
competencies in a highly secure
and flexible environment.

00:09:06.140 --> 00:09:09.010
Obviously, he still
wants to use renv

00:09:09.010 --> 00:09:12.490
to control the
dependencies and keep them

00:09:12.490 --> 00:09:14.390
separate for every project

00:09:14.390 --> 00:09:19.890
so different team members
can easily collaborate.

00:09:19.890 --> 00:09:22.480
So the last bit.

00:09:22.480 --> 00:09:25.660
I'm going to talk about is testing.

00:09:25.660 --> 00:09:28.880
And my message.

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:36.560
And my message
here is just do it.

00:09:36.560 --> 00:09:40.040
The only question is, what
tests and in what proportion

00:09:40.040 --> 00:09:41.600
should you be writing?

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:46.160
And we will use the testing
pyramid to answer that.

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:48.570
So you should aim to have
the most unique tests

00:09:48.570 --> 00:09:54.230
and data validation. The next
test components, a.k.a.

00:09:54.230 --> 00:09:57.530
testing Shiny modules, does the
whole scenario with end

00:09:57.530 --> 00:10:00.630
to end testing,
perform load tests,

00:10:00.630 --> 00:10:04.580
and finally test usability
with user interest in mind.

00:10:04.580 --> 00:10:08.920
Now let's quickly talk about
the tools you have available.

00:10:08.920 --> 00:10:16.690
Sor for unit tests,
I'm sure you all

00:10:16.690 --> 00:10:20.950
know the testthat package, so
just use it, but please set up

00:10:20.950 --> 00:10:25.240
your architecture early and
don't tell yourself an excuse

00:10:25.240 --> 00:10:28.120
to do it later,
because that probably

00:10:28.120 --> 00:10:31.850
won't happen because your Shiny
application would be awesome

00:10:31.850 --> 00:10:34.270
and your clients or
your business partners

00:10:34.270 --> 00:10:36.310
would like to move
on to something else.

00:10:36.310 --> 00:10:39.640
So start each project
with testing architecture.

00:10:39.640 --> 00:10:43.510
You already know that you
have a project template to set

00:10:43.510 --> 00:10:44.920
up at the beginning.

00:10:44.920 --> 00:10:48.880
You also know that you can
use continuous integration

00:10:48.880 --> 00:10:51.410
to trigger the
test automatically

00:10:51.410 --> 00:10:53.980
so you don't have
to do it manually.

00:10:53.980 --> 00:10:57.490
And please write at least some
tests for each piece of code

00:10:57.490 --> 00:11:02.810
and it would be easier to
extend them later. And obviously,

00:11:02.810 --> 00:11:04.550
keep your standards high,

00:11:04.550 --> 00:11:10.210
so do not accept pull requests that
don't include tests or actually

00:11:10.210 --> 00:11:12.940
break them.

00:11:12.940 --> 00:11:21.850
And so now it's the part about data
validation, and

00:11:21.850 --> 00:11:22.910
this is the logic.

00:11:22.910 --> 00:11:25.750
This is the
functionality that you

00:11:25.750 --> 00:11:30.010
should be doing on the same
level as the unique tests.

00:11:30.010 --> 00:11:33.550
So as we all know
from our experience,

00:11:33.550 --> 00:11:36.260
data can be very messy.

00:11:36.260 --> 00:11:38.590
And actually kind
of users report

00:11:38.590 --> 00:11:42.940
that this is their
biggest pain is the data.

00:11:42.940 --> 00:11:47.590
So I want to introduce you
to a data validation package, which

00:11:47.590 --> 00:11:53.500
is a tool for creating reports
based on the ROpenSci assetr's

00:11:53.500 --> 00:11:59.410
validation results, and
it allows you to create

00:11:59.410 --> 00:12:03.730
user friendly reports that can
be generated automatically,

00:12:03.730 --> 00:12:08.260
for example, with
RStudio Connect.

00:12:08.260 --> 00:12:11.170
So how can you use
data validation package

00:12:11.170 --> 00:12:12.430
introduction?

00:12:12.430 --> 00:12:15.790
Here's the example workflow.

00:12:15.790 --> 00:12:19.690
So you can run the RStudio Connect
Scheduler daily.

00:12:19.690 --> 00:12:23.950
And the scheduler can source
the data from a database

00:12:23.950 --> 00:12:29.680
and validate the results. Based
on the validation results

00:12:29.680 --> 00:12:33.280
a new data validator
report is created.

00:12:33.280 --> 00:12:35.570
And we have two scenarios here.

00:12:35.570 --> 00:12:37.630
So the validation fails.

00:12:37.630 --> 00:12:41.560
In that case, a
report can be sent

00:12:41.560 --> 00:12:43.910
to responsible stakeholders.

00:12:43.910 --> 00:12:47.470
So a responsible
person can actually

00:12:47.470 --> 00:12:49.720
take action and fix things.

00:12:49.720 --> 00:12:51.550
And obviously, on
the other hand,

00:12:51.550 --> 00:12:53.360
we also have all the scenarios.

00:12:53.360 --> 00:12:54.610
So everything is great.

00:12:54.610 --> 00:12:58.770
So the Shiny app can be
refreshed with the new data.

00:13:02.670 --> 00:13:06.360
And the next step that
I'm going to cover

00:13:06.360 --> 00:13:12.060
is testing, so the
face when you are actually

00:13:12.060 --> 00:13:15.480
testing the whole
components in your app

00:13:15.480 --> 00:13:20.280
and luckily, we have the shiny
test package from RStudio.

00:13:20.280 --> 00:13:22.950
And that it's easy to
use for developers.

00:13:22.950 --> 00:13:25.000
You can record the scenarios.

00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:28.710
And this provides a quick
and simple way to test your Shiny apps.

00:13:28.710 --> 00:13:31.600
On the other hand,
for more complex apps.

00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:35.010
We recommend using Cypress,

00:13:35.010 --> 00:13:41.100
but please be aware that this
requires knowledge of a little JavaScript.

00:13:41.100 --> 00:13:42.955
But it's really,
really powerful.

00:13:45.550 --> 00:13:51.040
And we reached the point that
your application actually

00:13:51.040 --> 00:13:55.210
is ready to go live, so you
want to make sure that is

00:13:55.210 --> 00:13:58.180
able to handle the traffic.

00:13:58.180 --> 00:13:59.780
So here

00:13:59.780 --> 00:14:03.100
you can use the shiny load
test package that enables

00:14:03.100 --> 00:14:07.390
you to load test your deployed apps.

00:14:07.390 --> 00:14:11.200
You can estimate how many
users your app can support,

00:14:11.200 --> 00:14:13.300
you can identify
bottlenecks, and you

00:14:13.300 --> 00:14:17.860
can use the test output to
guide you through the changes

00:14:17.860 --> 00:14:21.970
if if the optimization
is actually necessary.

00:14:21.970 --> 00:14:26.090
OK, and the last bit that you
really have to think about:

00:14:26.090 --> 00:14:30.430
so you also want to make sure
that what you're actually

00:14:30.430 --> 00:14:32.530
building is useful
for your users.

00:14:32.530 --> 00:14:37.750
So please talk
to them and you

00:14:37.750 --> 00:14:42.250
don't need to have a big budget
to do that because you can

00:14:42.250 --> 00:14:44.050
always use the Hallway Test.

00:14:44.050 --> 00:14:47.680
And the idea here
is that you can

00:14:47.680 --> 00:14:51.580
show the application to your
colleagues or to end users

00:14:51.580 --> 00:14:53.950
and let them click
through it and they

00:14:53.950 --> 00:14:56.590
can describe what they
are doing and what

00:14:56.590 --> 00:14:58.240
they understand is happening.

00:14:58.240 --> 00:15:02.470
And recent studies show that if
you do it to five people,

00:15:02.470 --> 00:15:08.230
you will learn around 85%
of the usability problems.

00:15:08.230 --> 00:15:11.680
And obviously, if you
don't do it at all,

00:15:11.680 --> 00:15:13.785
you will learn zero problems.

00:15:17.240 --> 00:15:20.480
So the things I want
you to take home: organize

00:15:20.480 --> 00:15:24.350
your code with Shiny modules,
R6 classes, or modules,

00:15:24.350 --> 00:15:27.200
depending on the app
complexity you see

00:15:27.200 --> 00:15:30.260
to run the length
and tests, you have

00:15:30.260 --> 00:15:35.450
to use renv control your dependencies,
and automate code testing

00:15:35.450 --> 00:15:38.530
and validations.

00:15:38.530 --> 00:15:40.650
Thank you very much.

